==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|ye}}, {{m|enm|ȝe}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ġē|t=ye}}, the nominative case of the second-person plural personal pronoun, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*jiʀ}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*jīz}}, a North-West variant of {{inh|en|gem-pro|*jūz|t=ye}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*yūs}}, {{m|ine-pro|*yū́|t=ye}}, plural of {{m|ine-pro|*túh₂}}. Cognate with {{cog|sco|ye|t=ye}}, {{cog|stq|jie}}, {{cog|nl|gij}}, {{m|nl|ge}}, {{m|nl|jij}}, {{m|nl|je||ye}}, {{cog|nds|ji}}, {{m|nds|jie|t=ye}}, {{cog|de|ihr|t=ye}}, {{cog|da|-}} and {{cog|sv|-}} {{m|da|I|t=ye}}, {{cog|is|ér|t=ye}}. See also {{m|en|you}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{enPR|yē|a=UK,US}}, {{IPA|en|/jiː/|[jɪi]|[ji(ː)]}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-ye.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|iː|s=1}}

====Pronoun====
{{en-pron|desc=personal pronoun}}

# {{lb|en|archaic|outside|Northern England|Cornwall|Ireland|Newfoundland}} {{cap|you}} (the people being addressed).
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|year=1596.2|book=VI|canto=XII|stanza=17|page=512|passage= My liefe (ſayd ſhe) '''ye''' know, that long ygo, / Whileſt '''ye''' in durance dwelt, '''ye''' to me gaue / A little mayde, the which '''ye''' chylded tho ; / The ſame againe if now '''ye''' liſt to haue, / The ſame is yonder Lady, whom high God did ſaue.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Elisha Coles |title= ΧΡΙΣΤΟΛΟΓΙΑ: Or, a Metrical Paraphraſe on the Hiſtory of Our Lord and Saviour Jeſus Chriſt : Dedicated to His Univerſal Church|year=1671|chapter=6|url= https://books.google.co.nz/books?id=U3kc5d4rYsEC|passage=Queſtion me then no more; whate'er '''ye''' want, / Ask in my Name, and God ſhall ſurely grant. / You've asked nothing yet for Jesus sake : / Ask and receive, and of my joyes partake.}}
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|year=1995|author=w:Elizabeth II|title=Legal Notice 247 of 1996|journal=Hong Kong Government Gazette|url=https://www.elegislation.gov.hk/hk/1996/ln247!en|page=B1096|text=Know '''Ye''' that We have declared and by these Presents do declare our Will and Pleasure as follows—{{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} {{cap|you}} (the singular person being addressed).
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1851
|author=w:Herman Melville
|title=w:Moby Dick
|section=chapter 23
|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2701
|passage=Know '''ye''' now, Bulkington? Glimpses do '''ye''' seem to see of that mortally intolerable truth; that all deep, earnest thinking is but the intrepid effort of the soul to keep the open independence of her sea; [...]}}

=====Usage notes=====
{{m|en|ye|Ye}} was originally used only for the nominative case (as the subject), and only for the second-person plural. Later, {{m|en|ye}} was used as a subject or an object, either singular or plural, which is the way that {{m|en|you}} is used today. In modern Hiberno-English usage, {{m|en|ye}} is used as a subject or an object in the plural, to contrast with {{m|en|you}} (singular).

